        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Measurement context indicating values refer to fishery catch.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/MeasurementContextScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Catch context</skos:prefLabel>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/CatchYearBasis -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/CatchYearBasis">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from GC DFO Salmon Ontology release 0.0.8 term gcdfo:CatchYear, modeled there as a SKOS concept.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://github.com/dfo-pacific-science/dfo-salmon-ontology/blob/c7a54251eb8f673052fed61f9ca2624e557249c7/docs/releases/0.0.8/gcdfo.ttl"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/YearBasis"/>
        <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gcdfo/salmon#CatchYear"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A year-reference basis in which a calendar year identifies when the represented fishery catch occurred.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/YearBasisScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Catch-year basis</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Use smn:catchYear for the xsd:gYear coordinate. A catch year is not interchangeable with the return year of the fish.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/CountStatisticalModifier -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/CountStatisticalModifier">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/iadopt/ont/StatisticalModifier"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The reported value is the number of summarized observations or individuals, as counted rather than estimated.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/StatisticalModifierScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Count</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/StatisticalModifierScheme"/>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">The construct, and the invariance condition it depends on, are the broodline of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, Conservation Units for Pacific Salmon under the Wild Salmon Policy, DFO Canadian Science Advisory Secretariat Research Document 2007/070, glossary: in a semelparous species, if the age of reproduction is fixed or nearly so, the fish spawning in sequential generations are called broodlines, and in Pink Salmon there are even-year and odd-year broodlines in most rivers. That document treats the two as separate races for establishing Conservation Units. The name cycle line is this vocabulary&apos;s, not that document&apos;s, which uses broodline throughout; see the scope note on smn:CycleLine.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/csas-sccs/publications/resdocs-docrech/2007/2007_070-eng.htm"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Broodline</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">One year series of a salmon population&apos;s returns, comprising the years congruent to one another modulo the population&apos;s cycle period under a declared year basis.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cycle line</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">Naming caution, recorded because it is unresolved: cycle line is this vocabulary&apos;s coinage. Holtby and Ciruna (2007), the framework this scheme draws on, says broodline and never says cycle line, and in Fraser Sockeye practice a cycle line is one of the four lines of the cyclic-dominance model — which is the model this concept defers. The term therefore currently names a construct in a scheme that excludes it. Cyclic dominance is a separate, deferred model and is not represented here: whether a line is dominant, subdominant, or off-cycle is a time-scoped status a line holds in one system, not the identity of the line. Its successor is named rather than left open — DFO&apos;s three Conservation Unit fields Cyclic (a boolean of the CU), Cyc_Dom, and Cyc_Dom_Year (which line, for that CU, in that period), whose values are dominant, subdominant, and off-cycle. Modelling those retires this deferral. Note that the fields cut across this scheme rather than along it: DFO records Fraser Pink as Cyclic = FALSE while still assigning it a cycle line.</skos:scopeNote>
        <skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme"/>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#ConceptScheme"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">The construct, and the invariance condition it depends on, are the broodline of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, Conservation Units for Pacific Salmon under the Wild Salmon Policy, DFO Canadian Science Advisory Secretariat Research Document 2007/070, glossary: in a semelparous species, if the age of reproduction is fixed or nearly so, the fish spawning in sequential generations are called broodlines, and in Pink Salmon there are even-year and odd-year broodlines in most rivers. That document treats the two as separate races for establishing Conservation Units. The name cycle line is this vocabulary&apos;s, not that document&apos;s, which uses broodline throughout; see the scope note on smn:CycleLine.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/csas-sccs/publications/resdocs-docrech/2007/2007_070-eng.htm"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Controlled vocabulary for cycle lines: the year series a population&apos;s returns fall into when the years are partitioned modulo the population&apos;s cycle period, under a declared year basis.</skos:definition>
        <skos:hasTopConcept rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Cycle line scheme</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">The construct is a year series, not a lineage. Reproductive independence is an additional claim that holds only where age at return is invariant, and it is asserted with smn:hasCycleLine rather than built into these definitions. Pink Salmon return at age 2 invariantly, so the brood-year and return-year partitions coincide and each line is closed under reproduction. Fraser Sockeye are the counter-case: roughly 89 to 92 percent return at age 4 but real age-3 and age-5 components exist, so a fish spawned on one line can return on another, the class is not closed, and the brood-year and return-year partitions of the same fish are not equivalent. Declaring a year basis from smn:YearBasisScheme therefore selects which assertion is being made; it does not reconcile the two.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/EnumerationMethod -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/EnumerationMethod">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/ns/sosa/Procedure"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">GC DFO Salmon Ontology release 0.0.2, term gcdfo:EnumerationMethod.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://github.com/dfo-pacific-science/dfo-salmon-ontology/blob/c7a54251eb8f673052fed61f9ca2624e557249c7/docs/releases/0.0.2/gcdfo.ttl"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">A method used to enumerate or count salmon in the field.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/MethodScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Enumeration method</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:topConceptOf rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/MethodScheme"/>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/EuropeanAgeNotation -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/EuropeanAgeNotation">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">Adapted from GC DFO Salmon Ontology term gcdfo:EuropeanAgeNotation.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.pac.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/fm-gp/fraser/docs/archiv-reports-rapports/indigenous-autochtone/2010FrasRvrChkInformDoc.htm"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/AgeNotation"/>
        <skos:closeMatch rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/gcdfo/salmon#EuropeanAgeNotation"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">Salmon age notation that records the freshwater-age component followed by the ocean-age component, commonly separated by a period.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/AgeNotationScheme"/>
        <skos:notation>European</skos:notation>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">European age notation</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">For example, 1.2 represents freshwater-age value 1 and ocean-age value 2 when the source declares European notation.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



    <!-- https://w3id.org/smn/EvenYearCycleLine -->

    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/smn/EvenYearCycleLine">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="http://www.w3.org/2004/02/skos/core#Concept"/>
        <obo:IAO_0000119 xml:lang="en">The construct, and the invariance condition it depends on, are the broodline of Holtby, L.B. and Ciruna, K.A. 2007, Conservation Units for Pacific Salmon under the Wild Salmon Policy, DFO Canadian Science Advisory Secretariat Research Document 2007/070, glossary: in a semelparous species, if the age of reproduction is fixed or nearly so, the fish spawning in sequential generations are called broodlines, and in Pink Salmon there are even-year and odd-year broodlines in most rivers. That document treats the two as separate races for establishing Conservation Units. The name cycle line is this vocabulary&apos;s, not that document&apos;s, which uses broodline throughout; see the scope note on smn:CycleLine.</obo:IAO_0000119>
        <dcterms:source rdf:resource="https://www.dfo-mpo.gc.ca/csas-sccs/publications/resdocs-docrech/2007/2007_070-eng.htm"/>
        <rdfs:isDefinedBy rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn"/>
        <skos:altLabel xml:lang="en">Even Year</skos:altLabel>
        <skos:broader rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLine"/>
        <skos:definition xml:lang="en">The cycle line of a two-year-cycle population whose years, under the declared year basis, are even-numbered.</skos:definition>
        <skos:inScheme rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/smn/CycleLineScheme"/>
        <skos:prefLabel xml:lang="en">Even-year cycle line</skos:prefLabel>
        <skos:scopeNote xml:lang="en">One half of the composite DFO Conservation Unit species code PKE, Pink-Even. Minted from the source code list rather than from observed data: DFO maintains even-year Pink Conservation Units, and a dataset holding only odd-year rows is a property of that dataset, not of the vocabulary.</skos:scopeNote>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>



